"context": "As a key work of satirical literature, "Catch-22" defined the genre's approach to critiquing social norms and institutions."}], "places": [{"name": "Pianosa, Italy", "role": "Setting"}, {"name": "New York City, USA", "role": "Publication"}, {"name": "Los Angeles, USA", "role": "Film Adaptation"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Catch-22", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "catch-22"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q278208", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catch-22"], "tags": ["literary work", "Novels & Prose Fiction", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Catch-22 contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": ""Catch-22" has shaped cultural perceptions of military bureaucracy and the absurdities of war, influencing anti-war sentiment and literary discourse since its publication.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-11T01:36:05Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#27317765776", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Novel by the american author joseph heller (1961) Created by Joseph Heller.", "newValue": ""Catch-22" is a satirical novel authored by American writer Joseph Heller, published in 1961, which critically examines the absurdities of war and bureaucracy during World War II. Set primarily on the fictional island of Pianosa, the narrative follows Captain John Yossarian, a U.S. Army Air Forces B-25 bombardier, as he navigates the illogical and paradoxical rules that govern military life. The term "Catch-22" has since entered popular culture, symbolizing a no-win situation driven by contradictory rules, reflecting the broader human experience of conflict and institutional madness.

The novel's plot intricately weaves together the lives of Yossarian and his fellow squadron members, presenting their struggles against the mindless military bureaucracy represented by Colonel Cathcart and the omnipresent, yet elusive, Major Danby. The eponymous "Catch-22" refers to a regulation that stipulates airmen can be declared insane and unfit for duty if they willingly continue to fly dangerous combat missions; however, if they apply for a discharge on those grounds, they are deemed sane and therefore ineligible to be relieved of duty. This absurdity not only encapsulates the futility of war but also critiques the dehumanizing nature of bureaucratic systems.

